"year":2001,"mm":-16.5},{"year":2002,"mm":47.8},{"year":2003,"mm":24.8},{"year":2004,"mm":47.5},{"year":2005,"mm":43.6},{"year":2006,"mm":3.1},{"year":2007,"mm":92.1},{"year":2008,"mm":98.1},{"year":2009,"mm":81.9},{"year":2010,"mm":104.2},{"year":2011,"mm":47.3},{"year":2012,"mm":132.9},{"year":2013,"mm":145.4},{"year":2014,"mm":118.3},{"year":2015,"mm":195.1},{"year":2016,"mm":263.9},{"year":2017,"mm":222.8},{"year":2018,"mm":188.5},{"year":2019,"mm":297.0},{"year":2020,"mm":264.5},{"year":2021,"mm":226.4},{"year":2022,"mm":188.9},{"year":2023,"mm":201.9},{"year":2024,"mm":252.1},{"year":2025,"mm":194.4}],"trend":null}],"places":[{"key":"galveston","name":"Galveston Island","gauge":"8771450","notes":[{"text":"Ike put 10\u201315 ft of surge on Galveston Island and west Galveston Bay in 2008, and 15\u201320 ft across the channel on Bolivar Peninsula. Every inch the gauge gains is an inch the next surge starts from.","source":"NHC Tropical Cyclone Report AL092008","url":"https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/AL092008_Ike.pdf"},{"text":"NOAA's 2022 report expects the western Gulf \u2014 Texas and Louisiana \u2014 to see the most sea-level rise of any U.S. coast, because the land there is subsiding while the sea rises.","source":"NOAA 2022 Sea Level Rise Technical Report, via Route Fifty","url":"https://www.route-fifty.com/management/2022/02/coastal-cities-expecting-greatest-sea-level-rise/362094"},{"text":"The planned defense is the Coastal Texas Project (\"Ike Dike\"): a gate across Bolivar Roads plus dunes on Bolivar and west Galveston, authorized in 2022 and now estimated near $57 billion.","source":"ConstructConnect (Feb 2026)","url":"https://news.constructconnect.com/massive-galveston-bay-barrier-system-moves-closer-to-construction-challenges-remain"}]},{"key":"buffalo_bayou","name":"Buffalo Bayou & the Ship Channel","gauge":"8770613","notes":[{"text":"Buffalo Bayou is tidal well inland \u2014 downstream of a point just west of the Shepherd Drive bridge, through downtown and Allen's Landing to the Turning Basin, where it becomes the Ship Channel. A higher Gulf raises the baseline of that whole tidal reach.","source":"Buffalo Bayou \u2014 Wikipedia (USGS runs a tidal gauge at the Turning Basin, 08074710)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo_Bayou"},{"text":"Harvey showed how the bayou floods: water peaked 3 m above predicted at Buffalo Bayou's upper bay reach after a \"triple punch\" \u2014 ocean surge, then Buffalo Bayou runoff, then the San Jacinto. Higher mean sea level was one of the ocean-side factors.","source":"Valle-Levinson et al. 2020, Science of the Total Environment","url":"https://repository.library.noaa.gov/view/noaa/50032/noaa_50032_DS1.pdf"},{"text":"The 52-mile Ship Channel is lined by 10 major refineries and dozens of chemical plants \u2014 and is also a conduit that can carry storm surge straight into a densely populated industrial corridor.","source":"ProPublica / Texas Tribune, \"Hell and High Water\"","url":"https://www.propublica.org/article/hell-and-high-water-text"}]},{"key":"houston","name":"Houston","gauge":null,"notes":[{"text":"City officials say only a small portion of Houston is at risk for major storm surge. The exposure concentrates along the bay and the Ship Channel corridor \u2014 where surge and bayou flooding can combine, as they did in Harvey.","source":"ProPublica / Texas Tribune, \"Hell and High Water\"","url":"https://www.propublica.org/article/hell-and-high-water-text"},{"text":"The land is sinking too. Groundwater pumping dropped some areas more than 9 ft within four decades, and Baytown's Brownwood subdivision lost so much elevation it was abandoned. Subsidence is irreversible.","source":"Harris-Galveston Subsidence District (Brownwood: ABC13 interview with HGSD, 2021)","url":"https://hgsubsidence.org/?p=646"},{"text":"Sinking has moved inland with growth: Katy has subsided more than a foot in about two decades, the fastest rate in the region, as northwest Harris County still leans on groundwater.","source":"ABC13 / Harris-Galveston Subsidence District (May 2026)","url":"https://abc13.com/post/new-data-shows-houston-area-suburb-sinks-more-foot-decades/19095321/"}]}]}}